Previous prompts from Meta ChatGpt and and even from 1st claude one had no conversation mode at all. After the student's attempts, the interaction simply ended with corrections. There was no defined path forward once the translation exercise was done.
In translate_chat of second prompt instruction from claude  — there is the biggest structural addition. After three attempts on a translation, the prompt doesn't just stop — it pivots entirely into a Sanskrit conversation practice mode. Specifically:

Model reveals the correct answer first
Then generates a thematically connected English sentence with its Sanskrit version already provided
That Sanskrit sentence is crafted to demand a response from the student — it's a question, not a statement
Student replies in whatever form they can (Sanskrit or English, correct or not)
Faults are ignored — the model responds naturally to the meaning, not the grammar
This loop continues indefinitely until the student stops

The intent is clear — once the formal correction phase exhausts itself, the student transitions from structured drill to living conversation, keeping them engaged with Sanskrit rather than dropping off after three failures. The topic stays anchored to the original sentence so the vocabulary feels continuous.
The gap worth noting: the chat mode has no re-entry into correction — a student who improves mid-conversation and wants to go back to drilling has no mechanism to signal that. It's a one-way door once chat mode begins.

